3. Noncommercial Restriction and Separate Authorization

3.1. "Noncommercial" means use that is not intended to obtain direct or
     indirect commercial benefit, monetary compensation, or business advantage.
     Whether an activity is free of charge, or conducted by an individual or
     an organization, does not by itself determine whether it is noncommercial.

3.2. Without separate written authorization from the relevant rights holders,
     commercial use of the Software or its modified versions is prohibited.
     Commercial use of the Software through an API-Only Independent Project
     is also prohibited. Examples include, without limitation:
     (a) selling or renting the Software, or selling access to it or its
         functionality;
     (b) providing paid calculations, subscriptions, memberships, paid group
         features, or other paid services;
     (c) monetizing related functionality or services through advertising,
         commercial promotion, or customer acquisition;
     (d) using the Software in for-profit products, business operations, or
         internal business support; and
     (e) receiving consideration tied to features, access rights, service
         quotas, or priority service, whether described as sponsorship,
         donations, points, or otherwise.

3.3. A voluntary donation is not commercial use merely because it is a
     donation, provided that it is not tied to features, access rights,
     service quotas, or priority service, involves no commercial promotion,
     and is not intended for profit. Donations must not be used to evade
     Section 3.2.

3.4. Commercial use, or an exemption from or adjustment to source-sharing
     obligations, requires separate written authorization from a Licensor
     authorized to grant the relevant rights. Its permitted purposes,
     duration, and other conditions are governed by that written agreement.
     Obligations not expressly waived remain in effect. A Licensor may make
     such arrangements only for rights that the Licensor is authorized to grant.

4. Corresponding Source

4.1. "Corresponding Source" means the human-readable, editable source files
     corresponding to the relevant modified version or version of the service
     actually provided, together with the project files, dependency lists,
     interface definitions, build scripts, deployment scripts, and example
     configurations necessary to build, install, run, and modify it.
     Executables, obfuscated code, decompiled output, screenshots, or change
     descriptions alone do not constitute Corresponding Source.

4.2. Corresponding Source need not include unmodified general-purpose
     development tools, system libraries, or general-purpose third-party
     dependencies lawfully obtainable under their own licenses. Necessary
     names, versions, and acquisition instructions must be provided.
     This exclusion must not be used to withhold necessary code that You
     authored, modified, or customized specifically for the relevant version.

4.3. Passwords, private keys, access tokens, users' personal information,
     actual business data, and third-party resources that You have no right
     to redistribute may be excluded. Provide example configurations, format
     descriptions, and lawful acquisition instructions in their place so
     recipients can build and run the corresponding version after obtaining
     the necessary lawful dependencies and resources themselves. This
     exclusion must not be used to conceal program logic that must be provided.

5. Public Source for Modifications to the Software

5.1. You must make the complete Corresponding Source for every modification
     You make to the Software publicly available. This obligation does not
     depend on distribution, release of executables, provision of services
     to others, or actual profit. It also applies to modifications used only
     locally or internally within an organization, and to modifications that
     are never distributed. Modifications include additions, deletions, and
     changes to code covered by this License, and modified versions formed
     by integrating that code into other programs.

5.2. The publication obligation arises when You make a modification. You must
     promptly publish its source through a public code repository or another
     stable download location. If You run, distribute, or deploy the modified
     version first, publication must occur no later than that first act.
     Work in development, experimental work, and unreleased work are not
     exempt. You may fulfill the obligation for ongoing development through
     a public development branch.

5.3. Public source must be available free of charge, without requiring
     individual approval, a service purchase, a nondisclosure agreement, or
     membership in a private group. Identify the version on which the work
     is based, the modifier, and the changes, and provide a version identifier
     or commit history that identifies the corresponding version. The public
     location must remain accessible. If it changes, provide a replacement;
     briefly publishing and then withdrawing source does not satisfy this
     obligation.

5.4. The Software and modified versions covered by this License must remain
     under this License so that recipients may use, study, modify, and
     redistribute them on the same terms. You must not add restrictions
     prohibiting public source disclosure, noncommercial modification, or
     redistribution permitted by this License. This clause does not change
     third-party licenses or require the Licensor to merge Your modifications.

6. Projects Developed Independently Using Only an API

6.1. An "API-Only Independent Project" is an independently developed program
     that does not copy or incorporate protected implementation code from
     the Software and uses its functionality solely through HTTP, RPC, or
     another interprocess interface. Interface communication alone does not
     automatically make a project a modified version of the Software.
     Parts that copy the Software's implementation code, or link or embed
     the Software as a library within the same program, do not qualify for
     this exception and must comply with Section 5 according to their content.

6.2. An API-Only Independent Project need not publish its own source to the
     general public. If it is used only by You personally and does not provide
     services to others, this License does not require You to provide its
     source to anyone else.

6.3. If You use an API-Only Independent Project to provide services to others
     based on the Software's functionality, You must provide Corresponding
     Source free of charge to the users who actually use those services,
     whether or not fees are charged or registration is open to the public.
     Providing functionality through websites, applications, bots, proxy APIs,
     or other intermediary layers does not remove this obligation. Actual
     users include end users who use the relevant functionality through such
     intermediaries, not merely callers who directly hold MZQ API credentials.

6.4. That Corresponding Source includes the frontend, backend, business logic,
     integration code, and files described in Section 4 that form the API-Only
     Independent Project and are necessary to provide the relevant service.
     Source for other independent systems unrelated to that project and
     service need not be provided. You must not exclude necessary code of
     Your own by splitting modules or adding proxy layers.

6.5. Prominently explain how to obtain source in the service interface, help
     pages, or API documentation. Provide a download location or a usable
     request channel for source matching the service version actually provided.
     You may verify that a requester is an actual user, but must not impose
     payment, confidentiality, waiver of statutory rights, or similar
     conditions, or unreasonably refuse or delay access. A source repository
     or download restricted to actual users is sufficient; public access is
     not required. Public publication also satisfies this obligation.

6.6. Keep Corresponding Source available throughout operation of the service
     and retain source for versions previously provided to users. Upon
     discontinuing the service, notify existing users how to obtain source
     and continue providing those users with Corresponding Source for the
     relevant versions for at least three years after discontinuation.

6.7. Code of Your own in an API-Only Independent Project need not be licensed
     entirely under this License and may use separate licensing terms.
     Those terms must allow actual users to obtain, retain, and study the
     source free of charge and to modify it for personal noncommercial use.
     This clause does not require a grant of permission to redistribute that
     independent code to the public. Third-party code remains subject to its
     own licenses. The Software and any modifications to it remain subject
     to Section 5 and must not be placed in an independent project to reduce
     their public-source obligations.

6.8. This section changes only who must receive an independent project's
     source. It does not waive the commercial restrictions in Section 3.
     Receiving source for an independent project does not grant permission
     to use MZQ commercially.

6.9. If You deploy the Software and provide API access to third parties,
     Your access terms must expressly include the noncommercial and user
     source-delivery obligations in this section. Require callers to accept
     them explicitly before access and provide them with this License.
     For independent developers who receive no copy of the Software and only
     call a remote API, these obligations must be implemented through access
     agreements they accept. This License does not assert that API
     communication alone automatically confers copyright in, or access to
     source for, their independent projects.

